Skill create_ai_task {
  label: 'Create or change an AI task'
  description: "Use when the user wants AI work to repeat or to watch for something, even without saying “AI task”: a recurring report or digest, an alert when something happens, a regular review of AI conversations, a check on data or the workspace, always with a repeat or watch signal. Typical phrasings: “every Monday, email me revenue by category”, “ping #sales if any region drops 20%”, “each Friday, review all AI conversations and summarize the insights and common questions”, “every morning, check yesterday's orders loaded”, “keep an eye on unused dashboards”. Also use to update, edit or change this chat's draft or a task the user names (“update my revenue alert to 9 AM”). Do not use for a one-off answer, a send right now, or AI Blocks. A recurring send of a dashboard as it is, with no AI work, gets the card below."
  invocation: 'auto'
  disabled: H.git.is_production
  content: @md
    # Create or change an AI task

    An AI task is AI work that repeats on a schedule or watches for a condition, then keeps or sends its result. Settle every component with the user, save the draft, and report. Never run the task or send anything yourself.

    ## What becomes a task

    - **Yes:** the request repeats ("every", "weekly", "each morning") or monitors ("whenever", "keep an eye on", "let me know if"), and each run needs AI work: reading, comparing, judging, summarizing or deciding. Any kind of work counts, not only metrics.
    - **No:** a one-off job, however large ("review all conversations" once), stays an answer in chat. Don't create a task.
    - **Dashboard as it is:** a recurring send of a dashboard or chart with no AI work isn't a task. Ask one card: "Send it as it is, or have AI summarize it each time?" with "Send it as it is (data schedule)" and "Have AI summarize it each time (AI task)". For the first, tell the user to use the dashboard's own schedule and stop.

    Examples of recurring or monitoring work:
    - Digest: "Every Monday, email me revenue by category."
    - Alert: "Ping #sales if any region drops 20% week over week."
    - Conversation review: "Each Friday, review all AI conversations and summarize insights and common questions."
    - Health check: "Every morning, check that yesterday's orders loaded", "monthly, list dashboards nobody opened."

    ## Fill the components

    Ask the user (only what the request leaves unclear):
    - **What the task does:** the work, its data or scope, and anything vague that changes the result.
    - **When it runs:** day and clock time, or a daily/monthly rhythm. The cron entry is UTC: convert another timezone for it, and tell the user the time in their own wording.
    - **Where the result goes:** an email address, a Slack channel, or kept in Holistics. See below.

    Generate yourself, never ask: the task's label, its description, the acceptance criteria, the brief, and any other setting the user didn't mention.

    ## Ask the unclear

    Ask one `ask_user` card for anything that changes the result and has more than one reasonable reading ("a lot", "top", "sales", "the team", "morning"). Rules:
    - Cards only, in business words, at most 15 words per question and three choices.
    - Put your best choice first, labelled "(recommended)", when there is one. Show today's numbers or examples in an option when they help the user choose.
    - Never ask what the request already settled, and never add scope the user didn't ask for.
    - Ask related unclear points together; ask Where last.

    ## Where the result goes

    - If the request names an email address, a Slack channel or "email me" ("me" is the signed-in user, `${H.current_user.h_email}`), it's settled.
    - Otherwise ask "Where should the result go?" with "Send to email", "Send to Slack channel" and "Keep it in Holistics". For an alert or a request to send, offer only email and Slack; if the user gives no destination, ask again.
    - Whenever the destination is email or Slack, named or asked, load `add_ai_task_delivery` for the details. "Keep it in Holistics" doesn't need it.

    ## Existing tasks

    - A request that names a task and says update, edit or change it ("update my revenue alert to 9 AM") edits that task: read its file, change only what was asked, keep every other line, and report what changed. If no task or several tasks match the name, ask which one in a card.
    - If you recall an earlier chat or open an existing task that looks like this request, don't reuse it silently. Ask one card: "Change <label>" or "Create a new task". Never copy wording from another task.

    ## Write the brief

    The brief is plain prose for the run agent: what to do, over which data or scope, each clarified answer, the condition for sending if any, where it goes and any attachment (from `add_ai_task_delivery`). If the user dictated a result format (a table, bullet points, a tone), put it in the brief. If they didn't, say nothing about format: a run loads `write_ai_task_result` by itself.

    Write acceptance criteria that fit the task.

    ## Save

    Save two files (an edit changes them in place): the task `settings/ai/tasks/<name>.ai_task.aml` (name: the label in snake_case, unique in a folder listing), brief and criteria indented 4 spaces, no `'` in label or description, `<END>` = the line of two semicolons:

    ```
    AiTask <name> {
      label: '<Label>'
      description: '<one plain sentence>'
      brief: @md
        <the brief>
      <END>
      acceptance_criteria: @md
        <the criteria>
      <END>
    }
    ```

    In `schedules.aml`, one more entry (comma after the last), cron in UTC:

    ```
      AiSchedule {
        cron: '<cron>'
        run_as: '${H.current_user.h_email}'
        targets: [<name>]
      }
    ```

    ## Report

    Reply in a few plain sentences, no template: the task's label, what it does, when it runs, where the result goes, the acceptance criteria in short, and that it's a draft: it runs on schedule once the user clicks `Commit changes`, then `Publish`. After an edit, say what changed (old → new) and that it's still a draft.

    If saving keeps failing, say so, give the reason in plain words and what the user can do.
  ;;
}
